s: register the Bluetooth controller with the stack, after loading the stack

S:Startup-Sequence runs AddBTClasses and BTStackLoader but never
AddBTHardware, so the stack starts with no controller registered. btParseCfg()
then finds no hardware to configure, and BTStackLoader reports "Error loading
bluetooth.prefs!" -- which reads as a missing preferences file when the prefs
was never the problem.

The prefs only records *which* registered controllers stay enabled. The code
says as much: with no hardware data present it deliberately removes nothing,
"because this could render the system useless (no USB mice or keyboards to
configure the hardware!)". Nothing registers the controller in the first
place.

The order matters, and this patch originally had it the wrong way round.

BTStackLoader is what opens DEVS:Bluetooth/FWLoaders/*.fwl. Registering the
controller before it runs means the radio reaches BHS_READY before any
firmware loader exists, so bluetooth.library takes the path its own comment
describes as "a firmware loader bound after this controller was already up":
it uploads the patchram, the controller restarts on Launch_RAM, and the
bring-up is re-run from HCB_RESET against a chip that is still restarting.

Measured on a Pi 3, that failed the same way every boot:

    bthciuart.device/0 ready: AA:AA:AA:AA:AA:AA, HCI 4.1, LMP 4.1 (Broadcom)
    brcmfw: 167 records applied; controller restarting
    firmware loaded after bring-up - re-initialising the controller.
    init step 0 -> timeout (0xFF).
    initialisation failed at step 0 (timeout).

The radio never returns to BHS_READY, and btEnumerateHardware() only reports
hardware in that state -- so the Bluetooth prefs shows no radio at all. That
presents as "scanning finds nothing" when there was no radio in the list to
scan with. The AA:AA:AA:AA:AA:AA above is the BCM43430A1's unprogrammed ROM
address, which is the other half of the same story: without the patchram the
controller has no identity of its own.

With BTStackLoader first the loaders are registered before the radio is
brought up, the patchram is applied inline at the bring-up's own HCB_FIRMWARE
step, and the controller is started once. Upstream orders it this way too:
its Startup-Sequence runs PsdStackLoader and BTStackLoader and never adds
hardware by hand, because a USB dongle enumerates after the stack is up. The
late-bind path exists for that case, not as the normal one.

Output goes to a file rather than NIL: because this is the call that answers
whether the radio responds at all. AddBTHardware runs btEnumerateHardware()
and prints "okay!" or "enumeration failed!", and a BCM43438 answers HCI_Reset
from ROM with no patchram, so it does not depend on firmware.

diff --git a/workbench/s/Startup-Sequence b/workbench/s/Startup-Sequence
index b4f4c7b..a4ee7a5 100644
--- a/workbench/s/Startup-Sequence
+++ b/workbench/s/Startup-Sequence
@@ -136,9 +136,45 @@ If EXISTS "DEVS:USBHardware/usb2otg.device"
     AddUSBHardware DEVS:USBHardware/usb2otg.device >>"SDCARD0P0:usb-hardware.log"
     Echo "finished AddUSBHardware" >>"SDCARD0P0:usb-hardware.log"
 EndIf
+; Load the Bluetooth stack, then tell it about the controller that is present,
+; the same way AddUSBHardware does above. Without the second step the stack
+; starts with no hardware registered, btParseCfg() has nothing to configure,
+; and that reads as a missing prefs file when the prefs was never the problem.
+;
+; BTStackLoader goes first, and the order is the whole point. It is what opens
+; DEVS:Bluetooth/FWLoaders/*.fwl, so with AddBTHardware ahead of it the radio
+; reaches BHS_READY before any firmware loader exists. bluetooth.library then
+; takes the "firmware loader bound after this controller was already up" path:
+; it uploads the patchram, the controller restarts on Launch_RAM, and the
+; bring-up is re-run from Reset against a chip that is still restarting. That
+; timed out at step 0 on every boot, the radio never returned to BHS_READY, and
+; btEnumerateHardware() reports nothing -- which looks like a scan that finds
+; no devices when there was no radio in the list to scan with.
+;
+; With the loaders registered first, the patchram is applied inline at the
+; bring-up's own HCB_FIRMWARE step and the controller is only ever started
+; once. Upstream orders it this way too: its Startup-Sequence runs
+; PsdStackLoader and BTStackLoader and never adds hardware by hand, because a
+; USB dongle enumerates after the stack is up.
+;
+; The output goes to a file rather than NIL: because this is the call that
+; answers whether the radio responds at all: AddBTHardware runs
+; btEnumerateHardware() and prints "okay!" or "enumeration failed!". A BCM43438
+; answers HCI_Reset from ROM, with no patchram, so this does not depend on
+; firmware.
 If EXISTS "C:BTStackLoader"
     BTStackLoader >NIL:
 EndIf
+If EXISTS "DEVS:Bluetooth/bthciuart.device"
+    Echo "starting AddBTHardware" >"SDCARD0P0:bt-hardware.log"
+    AddBTHardware DEVS:Bluetooth/bthciuart.device >>"SDCARD0P0:bt-hardware.log"
+    ; AddBTHardware prints "failed" and nothing else. The reason is recorded
+    ; through btAddErrorMsg(), which is what BTErrorLog reports -- without it
+    ; the log says something went wrong and never what.
+    Echo "--- BTErrorLog ---" >>"SDCARD0P0:bt-hardware.log"
+    BTErrorLog >>"SDCARD0P0:bt-hardware.log"
+    Echo "finished AddBTHardware" >>"SDCARD0P0:bt-hardware.log"
+EndIf
 
 Run <NIL: >NIL: QUIET ConClip
 
